Phantom-WG on Hetzner Cloud

    Setup                  {{ setup_name }}, in {{ location }}
    Phantom-WG Modern      {{ modern_address }}, {{ hostvars.modern.network_address }} on the network
    Phantom-Frontmatter    {{ front_address }}, {{ hostvars.front.network_address }} on the network

    The commands of this guide run in the directory of the playbook:
    {{ playbook_dir }}
    The guide is kept there, in {{ setup_dir }}/guide.txt.

Dashboard

    https://{{ modern_address }}

    The certificate is self-signed. The username is admin. This command reads
    the password from the node:

    ssh -i {{ setup_dir }}/id_ed25519 root@{{ modern_address }} cat /root/phantom-wg/container-data/secrets/production/.admin_password

Clients

    A client is added in the dashboard, and its configuration is exported
    there. The configuration reaches Phantom-WG Modern directly, at
    {{ modern_address }}:51820 over UDP.

    To carry the client through Phantom-Frontmatter, add this block to the
    same configuration:

    {{ client_block | trim | indent(4) }}

    With the block, the MTU line of the [Interface] section becomes:

    MTU = 1370

    Phantom-Frontmatter reaches Phantom-WG Modern over the network of the
    setup, which carries packets of 1450 bytes. 1370 is the value tested in
    the field on Hetzner.

Shell

    Phantom-WG Modern is in /root/phantom-wg on its node, and
    ./tools/prod.sh help lists its commands:

    ssh -i {{ setup_dir }}/id_ed25519 root@{{ modern_address }}

    On the node of Phantom-Frontmatter, frontmatter-api ghost status reports
    the data path:

    ssh -i {{ setup_dir }}/id_ed25519 root@{{ front_address }}

From here on the setup is yours

    Both firewalls take their ports from every address, SSH among them.
    The rules are yours to narrow.
    The SSH key was made for this setup. Your own key goes on the nodes when
    you choose.
    Updates run on the nodes, with the commands of the two products.
    Hetzner bills the two servers and their IPv4 addresses for as long as
    they stand.
    The playbook deletes nothing, and a second run leaves the servers, the
    firewalls and the network as they are.
    To remove the setup, delete in the Hetzner Console the servers
    {{ setup_name }}-modern and {{ setup_name }}-front, the firewalls of the
    same two names, the network {{ setup_name }} and the SSH key {{ setup_name }},
    then the directory {{ setup_dir }}.
